'''''Chinowa''''' (about 1507 – 1589), known in [[Zong]] as Chiu Chi-hôa (周芝華), was the daughter of [[Hokta]], miko of the [[Akwesa]], and the wife of [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]], the merchant who succeeded her father as miko and who conveyed the territory of the Akwesa to the [[Zong colonial administration]] in 1535. She was born in the chiefdom, married into the merchant house that had settled at the bay of [[Oktalwa]], and died at [[Tamsui]] sixty-eight years later, in the colony that had been formed on her father's land.

She and her sister [[Oktisa]] are the only women of the [[Kawesa]] peoples whose names are preserved in the colonial record.

== Family and early life ==

Chinowa was born about 1507, the daughter of Hokta, the ruler of the Akwesa. Her mother is not recorded. She had one sister, [[Oktisa]], and no brothers: her father had no sons, and the chiefdom's succession followed the male line.

Her father's territory comprised the bay of Oktalwa and its surrounding country, on the northwestern coast of [[Lurandia]], and lay between the lands of other [[Kawesa]] chiefdoms that sought access to the bay. From 1511 the merchant house of the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]], founded by [[Chiu Bûn-hái]] of [[Huwei]], had operated a depot at the bay under a grant of land from Hokta made in exchange for ten pigs. The company assisted the Akwesa in their wars against neighbouring chiefdoms, and the territory under Hokta's control expanded in consequence.

== Marriage ==

In 1521 she was married to Chiu Khéng-gio̍k, the son of Chiu Bûn-hái, who was then sixteen. She was about fourteen.

The marriage was arranged between Hokta and the elder Chiu. It is recorded in the colonial papers as an agreement between the two men and not as a matter between the couple; the later narrative of the marriage as a romance is not supported by the contemporary sources.

The absence of a male heir made the match significant beyond the alliance it sealed. With no son to succeed him, the union of Chinowa with a man of standing connected to the company supplied the chiefdom with a figure who could hold its rule, and it is in that light that the marriage was concluded.

She was given the name Chi-hôa (芝華) on her marriage, and took the surname of the family she joined, so that she appears in the papers of the settlement as Chiu Chi-hôa. The name is formed from her own: the first syllable of Chinowa is carried in 芝 and the last in 華. Her husband's Kawesa name, [[Kengohka]], was formed in the same way from the first element of his Zong name. The two names are thus a pair, each rendering the other's bearer in the other's tongue.

The colonial papers use the Zong name and the Akwesa records, so far as they can be reconstructed, used Chinowa for the whole of her life.

== Chiefdom and the sale ==

Hokta died in 1527, during an outbreak of plague that killed many of the Akwesa and left the chiefdom much reduced. Chinowa was about twenty. Her husband succeeded to the chieftainship and became the ruler of the polity of [[Okheng]], and she became the wife of the ruler of the territory her father had held.

In 1535 the colonial administration acquired the site as the harbour for its northern presence. Chinowa's husband was paid 4,000 taels of gold and was at the same time threatened with invasion and with execution for treason should he refuse. The land conveyed was the territory she had been born in and would have inherited a claim to had the succession admitted women. She was about twenty-eight.

Her husband remained in the territory as its provisional administrator until 1544, when he was removed for his part in the [[Tamsui Plot]], and the office was dissolved and replaced by the governorship of the [[Sinchew Colony]].

== Later life and death ==

She remained at Tamsui. She had been brought to the bay as a child of the chiefdom and she lived there for the whole of the remainder of her life, through the division of the colonial territory in 1544, the arrival of [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] explorers on the western coast in 1564, and the political upheavals in Zong of the 1570s and 1580s.

She died at Tamsui in 1589, aged about 82. She had been married for sixty-eight years.

She was survived by her husband by nine years; he died at Tamsui in 1598, aged 93, having held the office of ambassador to the Akwesa for fifty-five years. Their two children are not named in the colonial papers.

== Historical record ==

Chinowa appears in the colonial papers in three connections only: as the daughter of Hokta, as the wife of the man who conveyed the territory, and as the mother of two children whose names are not given. No document written by her or at her direction is known, and no account of the Akwesa themselves survives.

The record of her life is therefore the record of the transactions conducted over her father's land. The marriage of 1521 and the sale of 1535 are the two events in which she appears, and both were concluded between other parties. The colonial papers note her existence in the course of recording what was done with the country she came from.

